Z||000000||fiero56||11-15-2000||10:04 PM||||I have a <B>huge</B> beef with a certain state law, but I will post that one later.<P>One of my other problems is with e-check. Yeah, I don't like paying $20 and all that, but the worst is having to get out of my car, and be forced by my government to allow someone else to get in my car, drive it(even if it is 15ft), put it on rollers, and run it through the gears. This just irritates the pi$$ out of me, and I need to have the SE tested before the temps tags expire in early December. Maybe I could use the Formula e-check paper and they wouldn't notice(I'm gettting an idea here...).<P>Anyone else have a problem with stupid little state laws?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||11-15-2000||10:11 PM||||Did anyone hear that the DOTs are working together to get more money for those who use "their" roads?  Supposedly, they are trying to do a satellite and GPS thing that reads the amount of miles people use on the roads.  They will charge people for the number of miles they drive.  They said that people didn't think it was fair that people got taxed the same as people that drove ten times as much.  This sucks!  It probably won't pass, but it's still retarded.   <BR>There are others, too.  <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||11-15-2000||10:34 PM||||In New Jersey I think the insurance companies write the motor vehicle laws.I have been told by my insurance agent that if I want to take my 88GT off the road for the winter I must turn in my tags,which I just got and paid an extra $50.00 for(MYTMOUS),and Get a receipt  before I can cancel my Insurance.I pay over $3000 a year for my 88GT,87 Grand National,and my wife's 89 Taurus.What really gets me mad is if you get a ticket for a moving violation you not only pay a heavy fine,but the insurance companies can rape you.A $78.00 ticket can cost over $1000.00 in increased insurance premiums.I better quit this post now before my blood pressure goes any higher.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||11-15-2000||10:44 PM||||My father used to be a truck driver and I remember him telling me about some states out in the midwest that would stick his fuel tanks when he entered the state and tax him on what was already in them.Fuel that he had already PAID the tax on when he got it.<BR>(I'm on a roll tonight!)||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||11-16-2000||09:04 AM||||You're right as usual, fiero56.  I have a problem with stupid laws in general, whether they're local, state, or federal.  Like the fact that the Feds take millions of dollars from the states every year, then return them disproportionately as highway-maintenance funds...but blackmail the states into following federal mandates in order to get their money.<P>That's how they got everyone to adopt the 55 mph speed limit, and that's how they're getting states to adopt the 0.08 limit for drunk driving.  I've read that the average BAL in a drunk-driving fatality accident is 0.17 - nearly twice the current legal limit of 0.10.  So why not increase the penalties for the drunkest drivers, instead of cracking down on the moderately tipsy who are statistically less likely to cause accidents?  Granted, it's not a good idea to drive after drinking 4 beers - but isn't it a much worse idea to drive after 6 beers and a couple shots?<P>And don't get me started on e-check.  It's nothing more than a regressive tax on our poorest motorists and a pain in the a$$ for everyone else.||||reg||8
